{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And He spoke, etc., saying, I have heard, etc. Speak, etc.</b> It is difficult why the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> ('saying') was said in the first verse, and it was not sufficient with what was said afterwards: ״דַּבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״. And if the intention was that this detail too that He said to him — ״שָׁמַעְתִּי אֶת תְּלוּנוֹת״ etc. — he was to say to Israel, if so why was it necessary to say afterwards again ״דַּבֵּר״?" "And it appears to say according to the words of our Sages, of blessed memory (Yoma 4a), who said that anyone who says a thing to his fellow is under the prohibition of 'do not say it' until he says to him לֵאמֹר ('saying' — i.e., you may relay it). And behold, when HaShem spoke to Moses and said to him <b>שָׁמַעְתִּי</b> etc., these are words of HaShem to Moses, and this matter falls under the prohibition of 'do not say it'; therefore HaShem said to him <b>לֵאמֹר</b>. Now, there is no obligation in this that he must say it, but rather it is permission — He permits him to say it, thereby removing the prohibition — and the matter is in his hand whether to make known this word of HaShem to them. And when HaShem wished him to speak things that pertain to Israel, He said ״דַּבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, for from here onward these are things that I obligate you to speak. And therefore you will find that HaShem changed His manner of speaking, for at first He spoke in a hidden manner regarding Israel, as though speaking matters between Himself and Moses — except that He permitted him to tell Israel. And when he was to speak to Israel, he needed to speak to them in this manner — that HaShem said to Moses that He heard the complaints of the children of Israel — not that he was sent from HaShem to say to them in His name that He heard etc. But the second utterance He said to him: speak to the children of Israel on my mission — ״בֵּין הָעַרְבַּיִם תֹּאכְלוּ״. And the reason it was necessary to say the word ״לֵאמֹר״, the intention was that he should say to them in His name, may He be blessed. And this is the measure of the verse: speak to the children of Israel that I am saying to them, in the evening you shall eat etc., and this is His saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b>, and the word ״לֵאמֹר״ serves in place of כֹּה אָמַר ה׳ ('thus said HaShem'). Through this too the conclusion of the matter that He said — <b>אֲנִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם</b> — is justified, for without ״לֵאמֹר״ the matter would be heard, heaven forbid, as though said by Moses."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:55.793972820Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}